<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Calculating conditional probability from grouped frequencies</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>In a class of <b>{@Total@} learners</b>, <b>{@B@}</b> study Biology, <b>{@C@}</b> study Chemistry, and <b>{@Both@}</b> study both subjects. One learner is selected at random.</p>

<p><b>a) </b> Given that the selected learner studies Chemistry, find the probability that they also study Biology.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fully simplified fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Given that the selected learner studies Biology, find the probability that they also study Chemistry.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fully simplified fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>The conditional probability formula is \( \displaystyle P(X \mid Y) = \frac{P(X \text{ and } Y)}{P(Y)} \).<br>
When dealing with frequencies (counts of learners), this simplifies to dividing the number of learners in <b>both</b> groups by the total number of learners in the <b>given</b> group.</p>

<p><b>Part a: Given Chemistry</b><br>
We are given that the learner studies Chemistry, so our new total (the denominator) is the \({@C@}\) learners who study Chemistry. The number of these learners who also study Biology is \({@Both@}\).</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Biology} \mid \text{Chemistry}) = \frac{\text{Number studying both}}{\text{Total studying Chemistry}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Biology} \mid \text{Chemistry}) = \frac{{@Both@}}{{@C@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{Biology} \mid \text{Chemistry}) = {@ta1@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Given Biology</b><br>
We are given that the learner studies Biology, so our new total (the denominator) is the \({@B@}\) learners who study Biology. The number of these learners who also study Chemistry is \({@Both@}\).</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Chemistry} \mid \text{Biology}) = \frac{\text{Number studying both}}{\text{Total studying Biology}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Chemistry} \mid \text{Biology}) = \frac{{@Both@}}{{@B@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{Chemistry} \mid \text{Biology}) = {@ta2@} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
